<handout xml:id="day-30-lecture">
    <title>Day 30</title>

    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>Identify common failure modes of convolutional neural networks (CNNs).</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Explain how CNNs can learn spurious correlations instead of meaningful features.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Understand how pooling, striding, and other architectural choices can affect spatial information and translation sensitivity.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Distinguish between texture and shape information in image classification.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Explain how small, carefully chosen perturbations can cause neural networks to make incorrect predictions.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Describe approaches for improving the robustness and reliability of CNNs.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs component="slides-inst-notes">
        <title>Overview</title>

        <p> Convolutional neural networks have achieved remarkable success in image classification, object detection, medical imaging, and many other applications. However, high accuracy on a training or test dataset does not necessarily mean that a network has learned the features we intended it to learn.

            CNNs can exploit shortcuts, discard important spatial information, respond unpredictably to small transformations, prioritize texture over shape, and be fooled by carefully designed perturbations.

            In this lecture, we examine five failure modes. For each one, we will:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>Work through a small problem by hand to understand the underlying issue.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Examine a historical example from the research literature.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Discuss methods researchers have developed to address the problem.</p>
                </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs component="slides-inst-notes">
        <title>Lecture Schedule</title>

        <table xml:id="lecture-schedule" >
            <title>70-Minute Lecture Schedule</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="25%"/>
