                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Introduction: What Does It Mean for a CNN to Fail?</title>

    <p>A CNN achieves 99% test accuracy on cat and dog images. Has it learned to recognize cats and dogs?</p>

    <p>Not necessarily. It may rely on background features rather than the animals themselves.</p>

    <p>High accuracy does not guarantee reliable learning:
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>A model may perform well on familiar data.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>It may fail when inputs or data distributions change.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </p>

    <p>We examine five examples of how CNNs can fail.</p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Failure Mode 1: Spurious Correlations and Shortcut Learning</title>

    <image source="figures/shortcut-learning-examples">
    <shortdescription>Examples of neural networks exploiting background features and other shortcuts instead of the intended visual features.</shortdescription>
</image>
<p>
    Reference: <url href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-020-00257-z">Geirhos et al. (2020), "Shortcut Learning in Deep Neural Networks," Figure 1.</url>
</p>

    <p>A CNN may learn features that correlate with the labels rather than features that define the classes.</p>

    <p><term>Shortcut learning</term>: model relies on easy-to-learn patterns instead of the intended features.

    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>High accuracy on training-like data.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>Poor performance when spurious correlations disappear.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </p>
</paragraphs>

        <example xml:id="cats-dogs-backgrounds" component="slides-inst-notes">
    <title>Shortcut learning: Cats, Dogs, and Backgrounds</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Suppose we want to train a classifier to distinguish cats from dogs.
            We represent each image using two binary features:
        
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p><m>X_1</m>: The animal looks like a cat.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>X_2</m>: The image has an indoor background.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        </p>

        <p>The training dataset contains the following examples:</p>

        <table xml:id="shortcut-training-data">
            <title>Training Data for the Cat and Dog Classifier</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <row>
                    <cell>Image</cell>
                    <cell><m>X_1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>X_2</m></cell>
                    <cell>Label</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>Cat 1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Cat 2</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Cat 3</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Dog 1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Dog 2</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Dog 3</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>Consider two possible classifiers:

        <md>
            f_1(X_1,X_2)=X_1
        </md>
        <md>
            f_2(X_1,X_2)=X_2
        </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            Both classifiers achieve 100% accuracy on the training data.
        </p>

        <p>Now consider two new images:</p>

        <table xml:id="shortcut-test-data">
            <title>Test Data with Changed Backgrounds</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <row>
                    <cell>Image</cell>
                    <cell><m>X_1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>X_2</m></cell>
                    <cell>True label</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>Cat outdoors</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Dog indoors</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </introduction>

    <task>
    <statement>
        <p>What predictions does each classifier make on the two test images?</p>
            </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                For the cat outdoors, the features are
                <m>(X_1,X_2)=(1,0)</m>. Thus,
            
            <md>
                f_1(1,0)=1,\qquad f_2(1,0)=0.
            </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                For the dog indoors, the features are
                <m>(X_1,X_2)=(0,1)</m>. Thus,
            <md>
                f_1(0,1)=0,\qquad f_2(0,1)=1.
            </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
        Which classifier has learned the feature that actually defines the class?            
        </p>
    </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The classifier <m>f_1</m> has learned the feature that
                actually defines the class because it uses animal appearance
                rather than the background. It correctly classifies both
                test images.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
        <p>
            Why could we not distinguish between these two classifiers using
            only the training accuracy?
        </p>
            
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The training data did not allow us to distinguish between
                the two features. Both were perfectly correlated with the
                labels. The model could therefore achieve perfect training
                accuracy by learning either relationship.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</example>


<example xml:id="shortcut-learning-hospital-backgrounds" component="slides-inst-notes">
    <title>Shortcut learning: Pneumonia Detection and Hospital-Specific Cues</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Suppose we want to train a convolutional neural network to detect
            pneumonia from chest X-ray images. The intended task is to identify
            visual patterns in the lungs that are associated with pneumonia.
            However, the images may also contain information about the hospital
            where they were collected, such as image markers, acquisition
            settings, or other hospital-specific artifacts.
        </p>

        <p>
            For simplicity, suppose we represent each image using two binary
            features:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p><m>X_1</m>: The image contains visual evidence of pneumonia.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>X_2</m>: The image contains a hospital-specific marker associated with the training hospital.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            Let the class label be <m>Y=1</m> for pneumonia and <m>Y=0</m>
            for no pneumonia. Imagine that, in the training dataset, most
            pneumonia images come from one hospital and most normal images
            come from another. The resulting simplified dataset might look
            like this:
        </p>

        <table xml:id="hospital-shortcut-training-data">
            <title>Training Data for the Pneumonia Classifier</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <row>
                    <cell>Image</cell>
                    <cell><m>X_1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>X_2</m></cell>
                    <cell>Label</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>Pneumonia 1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Pneumonia 2</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Pneumonia 3</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Normal 1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Normal 2</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Normal 3</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            Consider two possible classifiers:
        <md>
            f_1(X_1,X_2)=X_1
        </md>
        <md>
            f_2(X_1,X_2)=X_2
        </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            The first classifier uses evidence of pneumonia in the lungs.
            The second classifier uses the hospital-specific marker instead.
            Both classifiers achieve 100% accuracy on the training data.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now suppose we evaluate the classifiers on images collected at a
            different hospital. The new hospital does not use the same
            marker convention, so the relationship between the marker and
            the diagnosis is no longer present. Consider these test images:
        </p>

        <table xml:id="hospital-shortcut-test-data">
            <title>Test Data from a Different Hospital</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <row>
                    <cell>Image</cell>
                    <cell><m>X_1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>X_2</m></cell>
                    <cell>True label</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>Pneumonia A</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Normal A</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </introduction>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What predictions does each classifier make on the two test
                images?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                For the pneumonia image, the features are
                <m>(X_1,X_2)=(1,0)</m>. Thus,
            <md>
                f_1(1,0)=1,\qquad f_2(1,0)=0.
            </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                For the normal image, the features are
                <m>(X_1,X_2)=(0,1)</m>. Thus,
            <md>
                f_1(0,1)=0,\qquad f_2(0,1)=1.
            </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                The classifier using lung evidence makes both predictions
                correctly, while the classifier using the hospital marker
                makes both predictions incorrectly.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which classifier has learned the feature that is relevant
                to the medical task? Explain.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The classifier <m>f_1</m> uses evidence of pneumonia in the
                lungs, which is the feature relevant to the intended task.
                The classifier <m>f_2</m> relies on a hospital-specific
                marker that is correlated with the diagnosis in the training
                data but does not indicate whether a patient has pneumonia.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Why could we not distinguish between these two classifiers
                using only their training accuracy?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                In the training dataset, the hospital-specific marker and
                the presence of pneumonia are perfectly correlated with
                the class labels. Consequently, both classifiers achieve
                perfect training accuracy, even though only one has learned
                a relationship that is relevant to the medical task.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What could a researcher do to reduce the risk that a
                pneumonia classifier learns hospital-specific shortcuts?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The researcher could collect images from multiple hospitals
                and ensure that the dataset includes a variety of acquisition
                conditions and hospital-specific markers for both classes.
                The model should also be evaluated on images from hospitals
                that were not included in training. This external evaluation
                helps determine whether the classifier generalizes beyond
                the particular hospitals represented in its training data.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</example>


<example xml:id="shortcut-learning-wolves-huskies">
    <title>Shortcut learning: Wolves, Huskies, and Snow</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Suppose we want to train an image classifier to distinguish
            wolves from huskies. We represent each image using two binary
            features:

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p><m>X_1</m>: The animal looks like a wolf.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>X_2</m>: The image contains a snowy background.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            The training dataset contains mostly wolves photographed in
            snowy environments and huskies photographed in environments
            without snow. Let the label be <m>Y=1</m> for wolves and
            <m>Y=0</m> for huskies.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="wolves-huskies-training-data">
            <title>Training Data for the Wolf and Husky Classifier</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <row>
                    <cell>Image</cell>
                    <cell><m>X_1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>X_2</m></cell>
                    <cell>Label</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>Wolf 1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Wolf 2</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Wolf 3</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Husky 1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Husky 2</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Husky 3</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            Consider two possible classifiers:

        <md>
            f_1(X_1,X_2)=X_1
        </md>
        <md>
            f_2(X_1,X_2)=X_2
        </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            Both classifiers achieve 100% accuracy on the training data.
            However, <m>f_1</m> uses the animal's appearance, while
            <m>f_2</m> uses the background.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now consider two new images in which the animals appear in
            different environments:
        </p>

        <table xml:id="wolves-huskies-test-data">
            <title>Test Data with Changed Backgrounds</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <row>
                    <cell>Image</cell>
                    <cell><m>X_1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>X_2</m></cell>
                    <cell>True label</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>Wolf in grass</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Husky in snow</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </introduction>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What predictions does each classifier make on the two
                test images?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                For the wolf in grass, the features are
                <m>(X_1,X_2)=(1,0)</m>. Therefore,
            <md>
                f_1(1,0)=1,\qquad f_2(1,0)=0.
            </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                For the husky in snow, the features are
                <m>(X_1,X_2)=(0,1)</m>. Therefore,
            <md>
                f_1(0,1)=0,\qquad f_2(0,1)=1.
            </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                The animal-based classifier correctly classifies both
                images, while the background-based classifier incorrectly
                classifies both.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which classifier has learned the feature that actually
                distinguishes wolves from huskies?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The classifier <m>f_1</m> uses the animal's appearance.
                The classifier <m>f_2</m> has learned a shortcut: it
                associates snow with wolves rather than recognizing the
                animal itself.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Why might a neural network learn the background instead
                of the animal, even though the animal is the intended
                classification target?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The background is an easy and highly predictive feature
                in the training data. A neural network trained to minimize
                classification error has no inherent reason to prefer
                meaningful animal features over background features.
                If the background provides a reliable prediction on the
                training data, the network may exploit it.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</example>



<paragraphs>
    <title>Historical Example: Pneumonia Detection from Chest X-Rays</title>

    <image source="figures/zech-hospital-shortcut.png">
    <shortdescription>Chest radiographs and activation maps illustrating hospital-specific visual features that a convolutional neural network can use to identify the source hospital.</shortdescription>
</image>

    <p>In 2018, Zech and collaborators studied whether CNNs could generalize across hospitals.</p>

    <p>Training data came from three hospital systems:
        <ul>
            <li><p>National Institutes of Health (NIH).</p></li>
            <li><p>Mount Sinai Hospital.</p></li>
            <li><p>Indiana University.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Key findings:
        <ul>
            <li><p>Models identified the source hospital with up to 99.95% and 99.98% accuracy.</p></li>
            <li><p>Hospitals differ in imaging equipment, processing, patient populations, and acquisition protocols.</p></li>
            <li><p>These differences can create visual shortcuts correlated with diagnostic labels.</p></li>
            <li><p>A model may learn hospital-specific features instead of pneumonia-related features.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Performance across hospitals:
        <ul>
            <li><p>Internal test AUC: 0.931.</p></li>
            <li><p>Indiana University test AUC: 0.815.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Takeaway: A model can exploit patterns in its training environment that fail to generalize to new environments.</p>

        <p>
    Reference: <url href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed.1002683" visaul="doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed.1002683">Zech et al. (2018), "Variable generalization performance of a deep learning model to detect pneumonia in chest radiographs: A cross-sectional study."</url>
</p>
</paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>How Can We Address Shortcut Learning?</title>

            <p>Several approaches can help:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>Collect more diverse data so that spurious features are not consistently associated with a particular label.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Balance the dataset across relevant subgroups and environments.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Evaluate the model on external datasets collected from different institutions or environments.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Use methods such as Group Distributionally Robust Optimization (Group DRO), which explicitly account for performance across different groups.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
            </p>

            <p>One formulation of Group DRO is
            <md>
                \min_{\theta}\max_{g\in G}L_g(\theta),
            </md>
            where <m>L_g(\theta)</m> is the loss on group <m>g</m> and <m>G</m> is the collection of groups being considered.

            Rather than minimizing the average loss across all examples, this objective minimizes the worst-group loss.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs>
    <title>Failure Mode 2: Loss of Spatial Information Through Pooling</title>

    <figure xml:id="spatial-information-loss">
    <caption>Loss of spatial information through global max pooling</caption>
    <image source="figures/spatial-information-loss">
        <shortdescription>Two feature maps with activations in different spatial locations produce the same global max-pooled value, illustrating loss of location information.</shortdescription>
    </image>
</figure>

    <p>Pooling reduces feature-map size and computation, but also discards spatial information.</p>

    <p>Pooling can:
        <ul>
            <li><p>Reduce computational cost.</p></li>
            <li><p>Provide some tolerance to small shifts.</p></li>
            <li><p>Lose information about feature locations.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</paragraphs>

    <example xml:id="same-pooled-output">
    <title>Try It by Hand: Two Different Images, the Same Pooled Output</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>Consider the following two binary images:</p>

        <table xml:id="pooling-inputs">
            <title>Two Different 2 by 2 Input Images</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="50%"/>
                <col width="50%"/>
                <row>
                    <cell>Image A</cell>
                    <cell>Image B</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                \begin{bmatrix}
                                1 \amp 0\\
                                0 \amp 0
                                \end{bmatrix}
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </cell>
                    <cell>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                \begin{bmatrix}
                                0 \amp 0\\
                                0 \amp 1
                                \end{bmatrix}
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>Apply a 2 by 2 max-pooling operation with stride 2.</p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Compute the pooled output for Image A.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Max pooling returns the maximum value in the pooling window. Thus, the pooled output is:</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    1
                    \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Compute the pooled output for Image B.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Max pooling returns the maximum value in the pooling window. Thus, the pooled output is:</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    1
                    \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>What spatial information has been lost?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The pooled representation tells us that a feature was detected somewhere in the window, but it does not tell us where it occurred. The two original images are different, but their pooled representations are identical. The spatial location of the nonzero pixel has been lost.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Why might this be a problem in an application such as medical imaging or object localization?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>In applications such as medical imaging or object localization, the precise location of a feature may be essential. For example, the location of a tumor or an object within an image can be as important as detecting its presence. Once this spatial information has been discarded, subsequent layers cannot recover the original location from the pooled output alone.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</example>



       <paragraphs>
    <title>Historical Example: Small Objects Lost in Semantic Segmentation</title>
    <figure xml:id="semantic-segmentation-small-objects">
    <caption>Small objects lost in CNN-based semantic segmentation</caption>
    <image source="figures/semantic-segmentation-small-objects.png">
        <shortdescription>
            Comparison of an input street image, its ground-truth semantic
            segmentation, and an illustrative CNN prediction. The prediction
            misses a foreground person, demonstrating loss of small-object
            information during semantic segmentation.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
</figure>

    <p>In 2015, Noh, Hong, and Han documented limitations of CNN-based semantic segmentation on the PASCAL VOC dataset.</p>

    <p>Observed failures:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>Small objects, such as people, were sometimes classified as background.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Large objects received inconsistent labels across different regions.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Object boundaries and fine details were lost.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Why did this happen?
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>Pooling and downsampling reduced spatial resolution.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Coarse feature maps could not preserve precise object locations.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Upsampling could not reliably recover the missing details.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Takeaway: A CNN may recognize the presence of an object but fail to identify its precise location and boundaries.</p>

    <p>Reference: <url href="https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content_iccv_2015/papers/Noh_Learning_Deconvolution_Network_ICCV_2015_paper.pdf">Noh, Hong, and Han (2015), Learning Deconvolution Network for Semantic Segmentation.</url></p>
</paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>How Can We Address the Loss of Spatial Information?</title>

            <p>Possible approaches include:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>Use fewer pooling operations or less aggressive downsampling.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Use encoder-decoder architectures with skip connections, such as U-Net.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Use dilated convolutions to increase the receptive field without reducing spatial resolution.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Use spatial attention mechanisms to help the network preserve or emphasize relevant locations.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>


<paragraphs>
    <title>Failure Mode 3: Translation Sensitivity</title>

    <image source="figures/translation-sensitivity">
    <shortdescription>Two slightly translated versions of an input image and their feature maps after strided sampling, illustrating sensitivity to spatial alignment.</shortdescription>
</image>

    <p>Convolutions are translation equivariant: shifting the input shifts the feature map.</p>

    <p>However, pooling and strided convolutions can break this property:</p>
    <p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>Small input shifts can produce different feature maps.</p></li>
            <li><p>Predictions may change depending on an object's alignment with the sampling grid.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</paragraphs>

    <example xml:id="small-translation">
    <title>Try It by Hand: A Small Translation</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>Consider the one-dimensional input</p>
        <p>
            <md>
                x=[0,0,1,0,0,0]
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>and a one-position translation</p>
        <p>
            <md>
                x'=[0,0,0,1,0,0].
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>Consider the filter</p>
        <p>
            <md>
                w=[1,0,-1].
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>Use the standard CNN convention of cross-correlation, and consider valid operations.</p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Compute the output using stride 1 for both <m>x</m> and <m>x'</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>For stride 1, the cross-correlation outputs are</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    x*w=[0,1,0,0]
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>and</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    x'*w=[0,0,1,0].
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>The response shifts along with the input.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Repeat the calculation using stride 2.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>For stride 2, only every other valid position is sampled. The outputs are</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    [0,0]
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>for both inputs under this sampling alignment.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Compare the outputs. What changes when the stride is increased?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>With stride 1, the response shifts along with the input. With stride 2, the operation samples only every other valid position, discarding the response at the intermediate location. In this example, both translated inputs produce the same stride-2 output.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Why might a small translation cause a large change in the representation produced by a CNN?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>This example illustrates how subsampling can make a representation sensitive to alignment: a feature can fall between sampled positions and be missed. The precise behavior depends on the filter, stride, padding, and sampling alignment. More broadly, downsampling can discard information about where a feature occurs.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</example>

        <paragraphs>
    <title>Historical Example: Sensitivity to Small Image Transformations</title>
    <figure xml:id="cnn-translation-sensitivity">
    <caption>Sensitivity of CNN predictions to small image translations</caption>
    <image source="figures/cnn-translation-sensitivity.png">
        <shortdescription>
            An original cat image and a version shifted slightly to the
            right appear nearly identical, but the illustrative CNN
            predictions differ: cat for the original and dog for the
            translated image.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
</figure>

    <p>In 2019, Azulay and Weiss studied CNN sensitivity to small image transformations.</p>

    <p>Key findings:
        <ul>
            <li><p>Small translations can substantially change CNN predictions.</p></li>
            <li><p>Transformed images can appear nearly identical to humans.</p></li>
            <li><p>Predictions may change even when the object's identity remains unchanged.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Why does this matter?
        <ul>
            <li><p>Small changes in image acquisition should not change a diagnosis.</p></li>
            <li><p>Patient positioning or camera alignment may affect predictions.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
        Reference: <url href="https://www.jmlr.org/papers/volume20/19-519.html">Azulay and Weiss (2019), "Why Do Deep Convolutional Networks Generalize So Poorly to Small Image Transformations?"</url>
    </p>
</paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>How Can We Address Translation Sensitivity?</title>

            <p>Several approaches can improve robustness:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>Use translation augmentation during training so that the network sees objects at different locations.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Use anti-aliased downsampling to reduce artifacts caused by subsampling.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Use architectures designed to be translation equivariant.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Reduce unnecessary downsampling when spatial precision is important.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
            </p>        
    </paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Failure Mode 4: Texture Bias Instead of Shape Bias</title>

<figure xml:id="texture-versus-shape-bias">
    <caption>A cat-shaped object with elephant-like texture</caption>
    <image source="figures/texture-bias-shape-vs-texture.png" width="65%">
        <shortdescription>
            A cat-shaped animal with elephant-like skin texture,
            illustrating how texture cues can conflict with shape cues.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
</figure>



    <p>Humans often recognize objects by their shape, while CNNs can rely heavily on local texture.</p>

    <p>This can lead to misclassification:</p>
    <p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>A CNN may predict a class based on texture.</p></li>
            <li><p>It may ignore shape cues that suggest a different class.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</paragraphs>

        <example xml:id="shape-or-texture">
    <title>Try It by Hand: Shape or Texture?</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>Suppose we have a classification problem with two classes:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>Class 1: circles.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Class 0: squares.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        </p>

        <p>Each image also has one of two textures:
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>Stripes.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Dots.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        </p>

        <p>The training data contains only the following two combinations:</p>

        <table xml:id="texture-training-data">
            <title>Training Data for Shape and Texture Classification</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="30%"/>
                <col width="35%"/>
                <col width="35%"/>
                <row>
                    <cell>Image</cell>
                    <cell>Shape</cell>
                    <cell>Texture</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>A</cell>
                    <cell>Circle</cell>
                    <cell>Stripes</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>D</cell>
                    <cell>Square</cell>
                    <cell>Dots</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>Now consider two new images:</p>

        <table xml:id="texture-test-data">
            <title>Test Images with Conflicting Shape and Texture</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="30%"/>
                <col width="35%"/>
                <col width="35%"/>
                <row>
                    <cell>Image</cell>
                    <cell>Shape</cell>
                    <cell>Texture</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>B</cell>
                    <cell>Circle</cell>
                    <cell>Dots</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>C</cell>
                    <cell>Square</cell>
                    <cell>Stripes</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </introduction>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>What would a shape-based classifier predict for images B and C?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The shape-based classifier predicts class 1 for image B because it is a circle, and class 0 for image C because it is a square.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>What would a texture-based classifier predict for images B and C?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The texture-based classifier predicts class 0 for image B because it has dots, and class 1 for image C because it has stripes.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Why can we not determine from the training data whether the classifier will learn shape or texture?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Both features perfectly predict the training labels. Therefore, the training data does not tell us which feature the model will use.</p>
            <p>The model may learn the texture relationship because local texture features can be easier for a CNN to detect than global shape information.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</example>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Historical Example: Texture Bias in CNNs</title>

    <figure xml:id="geirhos-texture-bias">
    <caption>Texture bias in standard CNNs and increased shape bias through Stylized ImageNet</caption>
    <image source="figures/geirhos-2019-texture-bias.png" width="75%">
        <shortdescription>
            Cue-conflict examples illustrate texture bias in standard
            CNNs and increased shape bias after training with
            Stylized ImageNet.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
</figure>

    <p>In 2019, Geirhos and collaborators studied CNNs' tendency to rely on texture rather than shape.</p>

    <p>They introduced cue-conflict images combining the shape of one object with the texture of another.</p>

    <p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>Example: a cat-shaped object with elephant texture.</p></li>
            <li><p>Standard CNNs often classified images by texture rather than shape.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>The researchers introduced Stylized ImageNet to encourage shape-based recognition.</p>

    <p>Training on Stylized ImageNet substantially increased CNNs' shape bias.</p>

    <p>
        Reference: <url href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s42256-019-0118-3">Geirhos et al. (2019), "ImageNet-trained CNNs are biased towards texture; increasing shape bias improves accuracy and robustness."</url>
    </p>
</paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>How Can We Address Texture Bias?</title>

            <p>Possible approaches include:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>Train on stylized images to reduce reliance on local texture patterns.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Use shape-oriented data augmentation.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Include diverse visual representations and examples in the training data.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Evaluate models using cue-conflict images to determine whether they rely on shape or texture.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
            </p>
    </paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Failure Mode 5: Adversarial Examples</title>

<figure xml:id="adversarial-stop-sign">
    <caption>An illustrative stop-sign adversarial example</caption>
    <image source="figures/adversarial-stop-sign.jpg">
        <shortdescription>
            An original stop-sign image, a small adversarial perturbation,
            and a nearly identical perturbed image that is illustratively
            misclassified as a speed-limit sign. A magnified inset shows
            the subtle image changes.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
</figure>

    <p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>Small, carefully chosen changes to an image can fool a CNN.</p></li>
            <li><p>These changes may be nearly imperceptible to humans.</p></li>
            <li><p>They can cause the network to make a completely different prediction.</p></li>
            <li><p>Such inputs are called adversarial examples.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</paragraphs>

    <example xml:id="linear-classifier-perturbation">
    <title>Try It by Hand: A Linear Classifier</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>Consider a binary linear classifier</p>
        <p>
            <md>
                f(x)=\operatorname{sign}(w^Tx+b),
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>with</p>
        <p>
            <md>
                w=
                \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                1
                \end{bmatrix},
                \qquad b=-3.
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>Suppose the input is</p>
        <p>
            <md>
                x=
                \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                1
                \end{bmatrix}.
            </md>
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Compute the classifier score <m>w^Tx+b</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The original classifier score is</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    w^Tx+b=1+1-3=-1.
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>What class does the classifier predict?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Since the classifier score is negative, the classifier predicts class <m>-1</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Find a perturbation <m>\delta</m> such that the perturbed input <m>x+\delta</m> is classified as the other class.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>We want to find a perturbation <m>\delta</m> such that</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    w^T(x+\delta)+b\geq 0.
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>Equivalently,</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    w^T\delta\geq 1.
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>One possible perturbation is</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \delta=
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    1.1\\
                    1.1
                    \end{bmatrix}.
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>The perturbed classifier score becomes</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    (1+1.1)+(1+1.1)-3=1.2.
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>The classifier now predicts class 1.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>Find the perturbation with minimum Euclidean norm that reaches the decision boundary.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>To find the minimum-norm perturbation that reaches the decision boundary, we solve</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \min_{\delta}\|\delta\|_2
                    \qquad\text{subject to}\qquad
                    w^T\delta=1.
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>The minimum-norm solution is parallel to <m>w</m>:</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \delta^\*=
                    \frac{1}{\|w\|_2^2}w
                    =
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    1/2\\
                    1/2
                    \end{bmatrix}.
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>Its Euclidean norm is</p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \|\delta^\*\|_2
                    =
                    \sqrt{\frac14+\frac14}
                    =
                    \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}.
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>This perturbation reaches the decision boundary. A slightly larger perturbation in the same direction crosses the boundary and changes the predicted class.</p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</example>



<paragraphs>
    <title>Historical Example: The Panda-to-Gibbon Example</title>

    <figure xml:id="adversarial-examples-overview">
    <caption>How a small adversarial perturbation can fool a CNN</caption>
    <image source="figures/panda-to-gibbon.png">
        <shortdescription>
            An original panda image, a small perturbation, and the resulting
            adversarial image classified as a gibbon. A magnified inset
            illustrates the subtle changes.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
</figure>

    <p>In 2015, Goodfellow, Shlens, and Szegedy studied why adversarial examples occur.</p>

    <p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>A small, carefully chosen perturbation changed a panda image's predicted class to gibbon.</p></li>
            <li><p>The perturbed image appeared nearly identical to the original to a human observer.</p></li>
            <li><p>Small changes across many input coordinates can collectively alter a network's prediction.</p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Their work helped establish adversarial examples as an important challenge in deep learning.</p>

    <p>
        Reference: <url href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6572">Goodfellow, Shlens, and Szegedy (2015), "Explaining and Harnessing Adversarial Examples."</url>
    </p>
</paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>How Can We Address Adversarial Examples?</title>

            <p>Several approaches have been developed:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>Adversarial training: include adversarially perturbed examples during training.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Fast Gradient Sign Method (FGSM): generate perturbations using the sign of the loss gradient with respect to the input.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Projected Gradient Descent (PGD): iteratively generate adversarial perturbations while constraining their magnitude.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Certified robustness: use mathematical methods to establish guarantees that a model's prediction cannot change within a specified perturbation region.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>


    <paragraphs component="slides-inst-notes">
        <title>Putting the Failure Modes Together</title>

        <p>The five examples illustrate different ways that a CNN can behave in unexpected ways. Some failures arise because of the data, some because of the architecture, and some because of the geometry of the learned decision boundary.</p>

        <table xml:id="cnn-failure-summary">
            <title>Summary of CNN Failure Modes and Potential Solutions</title>
            <tabular>
                <col width="20%"/>
                <col width="30%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <row>
                    <cell>Failure mode</cell>
                    <cell>Underlying issue</cell>
                    <cell>Example</cell>
                    <cell>Possible solution</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Shortcut learning</cell>
                    <cell>The model learns a spurious correlation instead of the intended feature.</cell>
                    <cell>Hospital-specific features in chest X-rays</cell>
                    <cell>Diverse data, external validation, Group DRO</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Loss of spatial information</cell>
                    <cell>Pooling discards information about the location of features.</cell>
                    <cell>Two different images produce the same pooled output.</cell>
                    <cell>Skip connections, encoder-decoder architectures, dilated convolutions</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Translation sensitivity</cell>
                    <cell>Striding and downsampling can make predictions sensitive to alignment.</cell>
                    <cell>A small image translation changes the network output.</cell>
                    <cell>Translation augmentation, anti-aliased downsampling, equivariant architectures</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Texture bias</cell>
                    <cell>The model relies on local texture instead of global shape.</cell>
                    <cell>Cue-conflict images</cell>
                    <cell>Stylized ImageNet, shape-oriented augmentation</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Adversarial examples</cell>
                    <cell>Small input perturbations can change the predicted class.</cell>
                    <cell>Panda classified as a gibbon</cell>
                    <cell>Adversarial training, certified robustness</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs component="slides-inst-notes">
        <title>Discussion Questions</title>

        <p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>Can a model achieve perfect test accuracy and still fail to learn the feature we intended it to learn? Explain.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Which of the five failure modes is most directly related to the architecture of a CNN? Which is most directly related to the training data?</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Why is evaluating a model on data from a different environment important?</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Can data augmentation solve all five failure modes? Why or why not?</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Suppose a CNN achieves 99% accuracy on a medical imaging task. What additional experiments would you want to conduct before trusting it in a clinical setting?</p>
            </li>
        </ol>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>TL;DR</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
    <li>
        <p>CNNs are powerful pattern-recognition systems, but they do not necessarily learn the features that humans expect them to learn.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>High accuracy alone is not sufficient evidence that a model is reliable. We must also understand what information the model uses, how its architecture transforms that information, and how its predictions behave when the input changes.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>Developing reliable machine learning systems requires more than optimizing predictive accuracy. It requires carefully designed data, appropriate architectures, meaningful evaluation, and an understanding of the limitations of the learned model.</p>
    </li>
</ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs component="slides-inst-notes">
